  insert into public.modules (course_id, level_id, title, description, order_number) values
    (v_course_id, v_level, 'JavaScript Basics', 'Your first programming language. Store values, work with text and numbers, take input from a person, and put a small working program together.', v_module_offset + 1)
  returning id into v_m1;

  insert into public.lessons (module_id, title, description, youtube_url, order_number, learning_objectives, notes, practice_activity, knowledge_check) values
    (v_m1, 'What JavaScript Is and Adding It to a Page', 'The third language of the web — what it adds on top of HTML and CSS, and how to attach it to a page.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EfAl9bwzVZk', 1,
      array['Explain what JavaScript adds that HTML and CSS cannot do', 'Link an external .js file to an HTML page with the script tag', 'Print a value to the browser console with console.log'],
      'Watch the **"Link JavaScript to HTML"** chapter of Dave Gray''s course — the same instructor whose CSS course anchored Level 3. Navigate by chapter name, not timestamp.

HTML is structure and CSS is presentation; JavaScript is *behaviour*. It''s what makes a page respond — validating a form before it submits, filtering a list as you type, updating a total without reloading. You attach it with `<script src="script.js"></script>`, and where you put that tag matters: placed at the end of `<body>`, the HTML has already loaded by the time your code runs, which avoids a whole category of "element not found" errors.

`console.log()` prints to the browser console you met in Level 3''s DevTools lesson. It is not a leftover beginner habit — it stays the single most-used debugging tool in professional JavaScript. Get comfortable printing values constantly to see what your code is actually doing.',
      'Create an index.html and a script.js beside it, link them, and console.log your name. Open the browser console to see it. Then deliberately move the script tag into the head, reference an element, and read the error you get.',
      '[{"question":"What does JavaScript add that HTML and CSS cannot do?","options":["Colours and fonts","Behaviour — responding to input and changing the page after it loads","Page structure","Nothing, it is decorative"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"Why is the script tag often placed at the end of the body?","options":["It loads faster there","The HTML has already loaded, so the code can find the elements it references","It is required syntax","To hide the code"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m1, 'Variables: let, const and var', 'Store a value under a name so you can use it, change it, and reason about it.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EerdGm-ehJQ', 2,
      array['Declare a variable with let and const', 'Explain why const should be your default', 'Recognise var in older code and know how it differs'],
      'This lesson moves to SuperSimpleDev''s course — **"Lesson 5 — Variables"** — because it covers all three keywords including `var`, which most beginner courses skip. It''s also the sequel to the same author''s HTML/CSS course you met in Level 2.

A variable is a name pointing at a value: `let total = 0;`. `let` can be reassigned later; `const` cannot, and should be your default — not because reassignment is bad, but because a `const` tells the next reader that this value is settled, and the error you get when you accidentally reassign it is a genuine bug caught early. Reach for `let` only when you know a value will change.

`var` is the original keyword. You''ll meet it in older tutorials and codebases, and it behaves differently around scope in ways that surprise people — Module 2 covers exactly how. Read it, don''t write it.',
      'Declare a const for your name and a let for a counter starting at 0. Increase the counter three times, printing it each time. Then try reassigning the const and read the error message carefully.',
      '[{"question":"Which keyword should you use by default?","options":["var","const","let","Any of them"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"What happens if you reassign a const?","options":["It silently changes","You get an error","It becomes a let","Nothing happens"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m1, 'Strings, typeof and Data Types', 'Working with text — joining it, embedding values in it — and checking what type a value actually is.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EerdGm-ehJQ', 3,
      array['Join strings with concatenation and template literals', 'Use typeof to check a value''s type', 'Name JavaScript''s basic data types including null and undefined'],
      'Watch **"Lesson 3 — Strings"** of the SuperSimpleDev course. It covers concatenation, escape characters, `typeof`, multi-line strings, and template literals.

Template literals are the part to internalise. Instead of `''Hello '' + name + '', you have '' + count + '' lessons left''`, you write `` `Hello ${name}, you have ${count} lessons left` `` using backticks. Fewer quotes to mismatch, and it reads like the sentence it produces.

**On data types, one addition from me, because no beginner course chapter covers this cleanly.** JavaScript''s basic types are: **string** (text), **number** (all numbers, whole or decimal — there''s no separate integer type), **boolean** (`true`/`false`), **undefined** (a variable declared but never given a value), and **null** (a value deliberately set to "nothing"). The `undefined`/`null` distinction matters in practice: `undefined` usually means *you forgot*, while `null` means *someone intended emptiness*. `typeof` reports a value''s type, and it''s the fastest way to diagnose why a comparison isn''t behaving.',
      'Build a sentence with template literals using two variables. Then declare a variable without assigning it and print its typeof — then set another to null and print that typeof too. One of the two results is famously odd; note which.',
      '[{"question":"What do backticks and ${} let you do?","options":["Comment out code","Embed variable values directly inside a string","Create a function","Convert a number"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"What is the difference between undefined and null?","options":["They are identical","undefined means no value was ever assigned; null is an intentionally empty value","null is an error","undefined only applies to numbers"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m1, 'Numbers and Math', 'Arithmetic, operator precedence, and the built-in Math methods you''ll actually reach for.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EfAl9bwzVZk', 4,
      array['Use arithmetic and assignment operators including modulus', 'Explain operator precedence in an expression', 'Apply Math methods such as round, floor, and random'],
      'Two adjacent chapters of Dave Gray''s course — **"Numbers"** then **"Math Methods"**.

Beyond the obvious operators, two are worth calling out. **Modulus** (`%`) gives the remainder of a division, which is how you test whether a number is even (`n % 2 === 0`) or cycle through a repeating pattern. And **augmented assignment** (`total += price`) is shorthand for `total = total + price` — you''ll write it constantly.

Precedence follows normal mathematics: multiplication and division before addition and subtraction, with parentheses overriding. When an expression gets long enough that you have to think about precedence, add parentheses even where they''re technically unnecessary — the next person to read it will thank you.

One genuine oddity to know now rather than discover in a bug: decimal arithmetic is imprecise. `0.1 + 0.2` does not equal `0.3` exactly. It''s how computers store decimals, it affects every language, and it''s why money is often handled in whole cents rather than fractional units.',
      'Calculate the total for three items with 16% VAT added. Use modulus to check whether a number is even. Then print 0.1 + 0.2 and see the result for yourself.',
      '[{"question":"What does the modulus operator (%) return?","options":["A percentage","The remainder after division","The larger number","A rounded value"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"Why is 0.1 + 0.2 not exactly 0.3?","options":["A JavaScript bug","Computers store decimals imprecisely — this affects most languages","The numbers are strings","Precedence is wrong"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m1, 'Getting Input from the User', 'Take a value from a person and use it — the quick way and the real way.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EfAl9bwzVZk', 5,
      array['Collect input with prompt for quick experiments', 'Read a value from an HTML input element', 'Convert text input into a number before doing arithmetic'],
      'Watch the **"User Input"** chapter.

There are two approaches, and the chapter covers both. `prompt()` pops up a dialog and is perfect for learning — one line, no HTML needed. Reading from an HTML `<input>` element is how real pages do it, since a prompt dialog is intrusive and can''t be styled.

The trap here catches everybody: **input always arrives as a string**, even when the person typed digits. So `''5'' + ''3''` gives you `''53''`, not `8` — the `+` joined two strings instead of adding two numbers. Convert with `Number()` before doing arithmetic. This one behaviour probably causes more beginner confusion than anything else in the language, and now you know it before it happens to you.',
      'Ask for two numbers with prompt, add them, and print the result — first without converting, so you see ''53'', then with Number() so you get 8. Then rebuild it with two HTML input boxes and a button.',
      '[{"question":"What type does user input arrive as?","options":["Number","String, even when the user typed digits","Boolean","It depends on the input"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"What does ''5'' + ''3'' produce?","options":["8","''53''","An error","53 as a number"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m1, 'Building Your First Program', 'Put variables, input, and output together into something small that actually works.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EfAl9bwzVZk', 6,
      array['Combine variables, input, and output into a working program', 'Break a small problem into ordered steps', 'Debug a program using console.log'],
      'Watch the **"Your First Game"** chapter, where everything so far comes together into a real, if small, program.

This is the most important lesson in the module, and not because of the syntax. Until now you''ve written single lines that do one thing. A program is a *sequence* — get input, transform it, decide something, show a result — and learning to break a task into that sequence before you start typing is the actual skill. Write the steps out in plain English first; the code follows more easily than you''d expect.

When something doesn''t work, and it won''t the first time, `console.log` between the steps to see what each variable holds at each point. The bug is almost always a value that isn''t what you assumed — a string where you expected a number, or a variable that never got updated. That habit, formed now, carries through the whole path.',
      'Write a small program that asks for a price and a quantity, calculates the total with VAT, and prints a summary sentence with template literals. Add a console.log after each step to watch the values change as it runs.',
      '[{"question":"What should you do before writing the code for a small program?","options":["Choose the colours","Break the task into ordered steps in plain English","Memorise the syntax","Write the tests"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"What is the fastest way to find out why a program produces the wrong value?","options":["Rewrite it from scratch","console.log the variables between steps to see what they actually hold","Refresh the page","Change the variable names"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (module_id, order_number) do nothing;

  insert into public.modules (course_id, level_id, title, description, order_number) values
    (v_course_id, v_level, 'Control Flow and Functions', 'Make a program decide, repeat, and reuse itself. This is where a list of instructions becomes something that can actually respond to a situation.', v_module_offset + 2)
  returning id into v_m2;

  insert into public.lessons (module_id, title, description, youtube_url, order_number, learning_objectives, notes, practice_activity, knowledge_check) values
    (v_m2, 'Making Decisions: if, else if, else', 'Run one block of code or another depending on what''s true.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EfAl9bwzVZk', 1,
      array['Write an if statement that runs code conditionally', 'Chain conditions with else if and else', 'Explain what a condition evaluates to'],
      'Watch the **"If Statements"** chapter of Dave Gray''s course — navigate by chapter name in the video''s chapter list rather than by timestamp.

An `if` runs its block only when its condition is true. `else if` offers another condition to test if the first failed, and `else` catches everything remaining. The conditions are checked **in order** and the first match wins, which is why a broad condition placed above a narrow one will swallow it — put your most specific tests first.

This is a short chapter, and deliberately so: the interesting part is the conditions themselves, which the next lesson covers properly. Get comfortable with the shape here — the curly braces, the indentation, one block running and the others not — and the logic will land more easily next.',
      'Write a function that takes a score out of 100 and prints a grade: A above 80, B above 70, C above 60, otherwise F. Test it with 85, 65 and 40. Then deliberately reorder the conditions worst-first and see what breaks.',
      '[{"question":"In an if / else if / else chain, how many blocks run?","options":["All of the matching ones","Only the first one whose condition is true","Always exactly two","None unless else is present"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"Why should more specific conditions come before broader ones?","options":["It runs faster","The first true condition wins, so a broad one placed first swallows the narrower cases","It is required syntax","It avoids errors"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m2, 'Comparison and Logical Operators', 'The operators that build conditions — and the equality trap that catches every beginner.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EerdGm-ehJQ', 2,
      array['Compare values with ===, !==, >, <, >= and <=', 'Combine conditions with && and || and negate with !', 'Explain what truthy and falsy mean'],
      'This lesson steps across to SuperSimpleDev''s course for the **"Lesson 6 — Booleans and If-Statements"** chapter, because it treats this material at more length than the anchor does.

The headline rule: **use `===`, not `==`**. Double-equals converts types before comparing, so `''5'' == 5` is true — which sounds convenient and is a reliable source of bugs. Triple-equals compares value *and* type, and it''s what you should type by default. `&&` requires both sides true, `||` requires either, and `!` flips a boolean.

Truthy and falsy is the other idea worth holding onto. JavaScript treats non-boolean values as true or false in a condition: `0`, `''''`, `null`, `undefined` and `NaN` are falsy, and essentially everything else is truthy. That''s why `if (name)` reads as "if name has anything in it" — useful once you know it, mystifying until you do.',
      'Predict the result of ''5'' == 5 and ''5'' === 5 before running them, then check. Write a condition that''s true only when a user is over 18 AND has agreed to terms. Then test if (0), if (''''), and if (''hello'') to see truthiness directly.',
      '[{"question":"What does === check that == does not?","options":["Nothing, they are identical","That the types match as well as the values","That the variable exists","That the value is a number"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"Which of these is falsy?","options":["''0'' (a string containing zero)","0 (the number)","''false'' (a string)","[] (an empty array)"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m2, 'Loops: for, while and do...while', 'Repeat work without repeating yourself — and know how to stop.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EfAl9bwzVZk', 3,
      array['Write a for loop with a counter', 'Use a while loop when the number of repeats isn''t known upfront', 'Explain what causes an infinite loop and how to avoid it'],
      'Watch the **"Loops"** chapter of the anchor course.

A `for` loop is the right tool when you know how many times to repeat: it declares a counter, tests it, and increments it, all in one line. A `while` loop repeats as long as its condition holds, which suits situations where you don''t know the count upfront. `do...while` is the variant that always runs at least once before testing.

The failure mode to understand now: if a loop''s condition never becomes false — you forget to increment the counter, say — it runs forever and locks the page up. Your browser tab will freeze and you''ll have to close it. This is normal, it happens to everyone, and the fix is always the same: check that something inside the loop actually moves the condition toward false.',
      'Print the numbers 1 to 10 with a for loop. Then print them again with a while loop. Then write a loop that counts down from 5 and stops — and deliberately remove the decrement to see the freeze for yourself (close the tab to recover).',
      '[{"question":"When is a while loop a better fit than a for loop?","options":["Always","When you don''t know in advance how many repeats are needed","When looping over numbers","Never, they are identical"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"What causes an infinite loop?","options":["Too many items","The condition never becomes false","Using let instead of var","Nesting two loops"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m2, 'Functions', 'Package a piece of logic once, give it a name, and use it everywhere.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EfAl9bwzVZk', 4,
      array['Declare a function and call it', 'Pass values in as parameters', 'Return a value out of a function and use it'],
      'Watch the **"Functions"** chapter.

A function is a named block of code you can run on demand. Parameters are the values you hand it; the `return` value is what it hands back. The distinction that matters: a function that *prints* something and a function that *returns* something are different tools. Printing shows a human a value; returning gives the value back to your program so it can be stored, compared, or passed on. Beginners reach for `console.log` inside a function when they meant `return`, and then wonder why the result vanishes.

Naming matters more than it seems. `calculateVAT(price)` tells the next reader — usually you, in three weeks — exactly what it does. Functions are how programs stay manageable as they grow, which is why this chapter underpins everything that follows.',
      'Write a function that takes a price and returns it with 16% VAT added. Call it with three different prices and store each result in a variable. Then write a second version that logs instead of returning, and confirm you can''t use its result.',
      '[{"question":"What does return do?","options":["Prints a value to the console","Hands a value back to the code that called the function","Stops the program","Creates a variable"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"What are parameters?","options":["The function''s name","The values a function accepts as input","The return value","Comments inside a function"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m2, 'Scope: var, let and const', 'Where a variable is visible, and why the modern keywords behave better than the old one.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EfAl9bwzVZk', 5,
      array['Explain what scope means for a variable', 'Describe how var, let, and const differ', 'Choose the right declaration keyword by default'],
      'Watch the **"Scope - var, let, const"** chapter.

Scope is the answer to "where can I use this variable?". A variable declared inside a function exists only inside it; one declared at the top level is visible everywhere. That''s a feature, not a limitation — it means two functions can each have their own `total` without colliding.

`var` is the original keyword and it''s function-scoped, which produces genuinely surprising behaviour inside blocks like loops and `if` statements. `let` and `const` are block-scoped, meaning they respect the nearest curly braces, which is what almost everyone expects. Default to `const`, switch to `let` only when you need to reassign, and treat `var` as something you read in older code rather than something you write.',
      'Declare a variable inside a function and try to use it outside — read the error carefully. Then declare one with var inside an if block and another with let, and try to access each after the block closes.',
      '[{"question":"What is the practical difference between var and let?","options":["None","var is function-scoped; let is block-scoped and respects the nearest curly braces","var is newer","let cannot be reassigned"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"Which keyword should you reach for by default?","options":["var","const","let","It makes no difference"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m2, 'Arrow Functions', 'The shorter function syntax you''ll see everywhere in modern JavaScript.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h33Srr5J9nY', 6,
      array['Write a function using arrow syntax', 'Use an implicit return for a single-expression function', 'Recognise arrow functions when reading other people''s code'],
      'A short focused video from Web Dev Simplified on the syntax that dominates modern JavaScript.

`const double = (n) => n * 2;` does what a regular function does, in far fewer characters. When the body is a single expression, you can drop the braces and the `return` keyword entirely — that''s the implicit return, and it''s why array methods in the next module read so cleanly. With exactly one parameter, the parentheses are optional too.

The video also covers how arrow functions treat `this` differently from regular functions. That distinction won''t bite you yet, but note that it exists — it''s a real difference in behaviour, not just shorter spelling, and it becomes relevant once you''re writing objects with methods. For now, focus on reading the syntax fluently, because you''ll meet it constantly from here on.',
      'Rewrite your VAT function from two lessons ago as an arrow function, first with braces and an explicit return, then as a one-liner with an implicit return. Confirm all three versions give the same answer.',
      '[{"question":"What is an implicit return?","options":["A return that happens on error","A single-expression arrow function returns its value without the return keyword","Returning nothing","Returning more than one value"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"Besides being shorter, how do arrow functions differ from regular functions?","options":["They run faster","They treat this differently","They cannot take parameters","They cannot be named"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m2, 'Switch Statements and the Ternary Operator', 'Two cleaner alternatives to a long chain of if/else.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EfAl9bwzVZk', 7,
      array['Write a switch statement with cases and a default', 'Explain why break matters in a switch', 'Write a conditional assignment with the ternary operator'],
      'Two short adjacent chapters — **"Switch Statements"** then **"Ternary Operators"**.

A `switch` compares one value against several possible cases, which reads better than five chained `else if`s when you''re testing the same variable each time. The catch is `break`: without it, execution falls through into the next case and keeps going, which is occasionally useful and much more often a bug. Always include a `default` for the unmatched case.

The ternary operator is a one-line if/else that produces a value: `const status = score >= 50 ? ''Pass'' : ''Fail'';`. It''s ideal for simple either/or assignments, and it becomes unreadable the moment you nest one inside another — when you feel the urge to nest, write a normal `if` instead. You''ll see ternaries constantly in frontend code, so reading them fluently matters as much as writing them.',
      'Write a switch that takes a day number 1-7 and prints the day name, with a default for anything else. Remove one break and observe the fall-through. Then rewrite a simple if/else as a ternary.',
      '[{"question":"What happens in a switch if you omit break?","options":["A syntax error","Execution falls through into the following cases","The switch is skipped","Only default runs"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"What does the ternary operator produce?","options":["Nothing, it only branches","A value, chosen between two options by a condition","A loop","A function"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (module_id, order_number) do nothing;

  insert into public.modules (course_id, level_id, title, description, order_number) values
    (v_course_id, v_level, 'Arrays, Objects and Working with Data', 'Real programs juggle lists of things, not single values. Structure data into arrays and objects, loop over it, transform it, and hand it between systems as JSON.', v_module_offset + 3)
  returning id into v_m3;

  insert into public.lessons (module_id, title, description, youtube_url, order_number, learning_objectives, notes, practice_activity, knowledge_check) values
    (v_m3, 'Arrays', 'Store a list of values in one variable — indexing, length, and adding or removing items.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EerdGm-ehJQ', 1,
      array['Create an array and read values by index', 'Use .length to find how many items an array holds', 'Add and remove items with push and splice'],
      'Watch **Lesson 11 — Arrays and Loops** of the anchor course (navigate by the lesson name in the chapter list, not by a timestamp).

An array holds an ordered list in a single variable: `const prices = [50, 120, 75];`. You read items by position with `prices[0]` — and positions start at **zero**, which is the detail that trips up every beginner exactly once. `.length` tells you how many items there are, so the last item is always `arr[arr.length - 1]`. `.push()` adds to the end and `.splice()` removes from a position. One subtlety worth catching early: `.slice()` makes a *copy*, which matters because arrays are handled by reference — assigning one array to another variable gives you two names for the same list, not two lists.',
      'Build an array of five subjects you''re studying. Print the first and last using .length, push a sixth, then splice out the third and print the array at each step.',
      '[{"question":"What index does the first item of an array have?","options":["1","0","-1","It has no index"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"How do you get the last item of an array called items?","options":["items[items.length]","items[items.length - 1]","items.last","items[-1]"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m3, 'Looping Through Arrays: for and forEach', 'Do something to every item in a list without writing the same line ten times.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EerdGm-ehJQ', 2,
      array['Loop over an array with an index-based for loop', 'Use forEach to run a function on every item', 'Build a new array from inside a loop'],
      'This spans **Lesson 11 — Arrays and Loops** and the opening of **Lesson 12 — Advanced Functions**.

The index-based loop `for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++)` is the foundation — `i` counts up and `arr[i]` gets each item. Then `forEach()` does the same thing more readably: you hand it a function, and it runs that function once per item. Note the shape of that, because it''s the first time you''ll pass a function *into* another function as an argument, and that idea underpins everything in the next lesson.

A note on what you''ll meet elsewhere: JavaScript also has a `for...of` loop, which this course doesn''t use — it sticks with index loops and `forEach`. You''ll see `for...of` in other people''s code, and it reads as "for each item of this array"; there''s nothing to unlearn here, it''s just a third option.',
      'Take your subjects array and print each one with an index-based for loop. Then do it again with forEach and compare which you find easier to read. Finally, use a loop to build a second array containing only the subjects with more than six letters.',
      '[{"question":"What does forEach take as its argument?","options":["A number","A function to run on each item","Another array","A string"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"In for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++), what does i represent?","options":["The item itself","The current index position","The array length","A random number"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m3, 'Transforming Arrays: map and filter', 'Two methods that replace most of the loops you would otherwise write.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EerdGm-ehJQ', 3,
      array['Use map to build a new array by transforming every item', 'Use filter to build a new array of items that pass a test', 'Write these with arrow function shorthand'],
      'Watch **Lesson 12 — Advanced Functions**.

`map()` takes every item, runs your function on it, and gives back a **new array** of the results — prices to prices-with-tax, names to greetings. `filter()` runs a true/false test on each item and gives back a new array of only the ones that passed. Both leave the original array untouched, which is a genuinely important property: you can chain them without destroying your source data.

The video shows these first with full function syntax, then with arrow shorthand (`prices.map(price => price * 1.16)`), which is where arrow functions from the previous module start paying off. Related methods you''ll meet in other code: `find()` returns the first matching item rather than a filtered array, and `reduce()` boils an array down to a single value. The pattern is the same each time — hand the method a function, get something back.',
      'Take an array of prices in shillings. Use map to add 16% VAT to each, then filter the result to only those above 1000. Write it once with a full function and once with arrow shorthand.',
      '[{"question":"What does map return?","options":["The original array, modified","A new array of transformed items","A single number","Nothing"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"What does the function you pass to filter need to return?","options":["A new item","True or false","An array","A string"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m3, 'Objects', 'Group related information about one thing — a learner, a product, an order — under named properties.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EerdGm-ehJQ', 4,
      array['Create an object with named properties and read them with dot notation', 'Use bracket notation and explain when it''s necessary', 'Store a function on an object as a method'],
      'Watch **Lesson 8 — Objects**.

Where an array is a numbered list, an object is a set of *named* values: `const learner = { name: ''Amina'', level: 3 };`. Read them with dot notation (`learner.name`) or brackets (`learner[''name'']`). Brackets look redundant until you need the property name to be a variable — `learner[key]` — which is exactly what happens when you loop over data. Objects nest freely, so a property''s value can be another object or an array, and that''s how real data is shaped.

A function stored on an object is called a method, and there''s shorthand syntax for writing them. One thing to internalise, because it causes real bugs: objects are compared and assigned **by reference**, not by value. Two separately-created objects with identical contents are not `===` equal, and assigning an object to a new variable gives you a second name for the same object.',
      'Build an object describing a course: title, price, and a nested object for the instructor with a name and country. Read a value with dot notation, then read the same value using a variable and bracket notation.',
      '[{"question":"When do you need bracket notation instead of dot notation?","options":["Never, they are identical","When the property name is held in a variable","Only for numbers","Only inside functions"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"Two objects have identical contents. Are they === equal?","options":["Yes, contents are compared","No, objects are compared by reference","Only if frozen","Only if nested"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m3, 'Arrays of Objects', 'The shape almost every real application''s data takes — a list of records — and how to work through it.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EerdGm-ehJQ', 5,
      array['Build an array of objects representing a list of records', 'Loop over it and read properties from each item', 'Add and remove records from the list'],
      'Still **Lesson 11 — Arrays and Loops**, specifically the Todo List project. This is the most important lesson in the module.

An array of objects — `[{ name: ''Buy airtime'', dueDate: ''2026-09-01'' }, ...]` — is what data looks like almost everywhere: a list of products, of students, of orders, of messages. Every API you call in Level 7 will hand you this shape. The project builds it end to end: loop the array, pull properties off each object, render them onto the page, `push()` a new record when someone adds one, and `splice()` to delete.

If a single idea from Level 4 carries you furthest, it''s this one. Watch how the loop and the object access combine, because the rest of the path leans on that combination constantly.',
      'Build an array of four objects, each a course with a title and price. Loop over it and print one line per course. Then add a fifth with push, and remove one by its position.',
      '[{"question":"Why is an array of objects such a common data shape?","options":["It is faster than other shapes","It represents a list of records — products, users, orders — which is how most real data is structured","It uses less memory","It is required by JavaScript"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"Inside a loop over an array of objects, how do you read a property of the current item?","options":["list.property","list[i].property","property(list)","list->property"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m3, 'JSON and Destructuring', 'The universal format for moving data between systems, and a shorter way to pull values out of objects and arrays.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EerdGm-ehJQ', 6,
      array['Explain what JSON is and why systems exchange data in it', 'Convert between objects and JSON with stringify and parse', 'Destructure values out of an object and an array'],
      'Destructuring and JSON both appear in **Lesson 8 — Objects**, with array destructuring in **Lesson 11**.

JSON is a text format that looks almost exactly like a JavaScript object, and it''s how systems hand data to each other — every API you meet later returns it. `JSON.stringify()` turns an object into that text; `JSON.parse()` turns the text back into an object you can use. Here you''ll see it used for saving and loading data rather than for network calls; the API side comes in Level 7, and it''s the same two functions.

Destructuring is shorthand for pulling values out. Instead of `const name = learner.name;` twice over, you write `const { name, level } = learner;`. It works on arrays too, by position: `const [first, second] = scores;`. You can rename as you go (`const { price: cost } = product;`). It looks like unfamiliar punctuation for about a day, and then it''s the syntax you reach for by default — and it''s everywhere in the frontend code coming in Level 5.',
      'Take your course object, stringify it and print the text, then parse it back and confirm you can read a property again. Then destructure its title and price into two variables in one line.',
      '[{"question":"What does JSON.parse() do?","options":["Turns an object into text","Turns JSON text back into a usable object","Deletes a property","Sends data to a server"],"correct_index":1},{"question":"What does const { title, price } = course; do?","options":["Creates a new object","Pulls the title and price properties into their own variables","Deletes those properties","Renames the object"],"correct_index":1}]'::jsonb)
